WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.630
A beetle can climb one rung or two rungs in a jump.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.310
It starts on the ground.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:08.230
To reach rung two, it can jump straight there,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.770
or visit rung one first.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.810
Draw both routes with your finger.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.400
How many routes reach rung three?

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:18.540
Look backwards.

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.920
The last jump came from rung two or rung one.

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.290
Each route to rung two has one way to finish with

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.040
a short jump.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.950
The single route to rung one can finish with

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:29.660
a long jump.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:31.040
That makes three.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:33.610
Now the third rung breaks.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.070
The beetle may fly over the gap,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.630
but it cannot land there.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:39.890
Does rung four become unreachable?

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.110
Find the last safe place the beetle could have landed.

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:49.700
To land on four, the beetle must come from two.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:51.720
A jump from three is impossible.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.340
Both routes to two can cross the gap.

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.650
Trace their different beginnings and their

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.060
identical last jump.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:00.140
Write two routes beside four.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.230
At every safe rung, add the counts at the

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:04.380
two previous positions.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:06.760
Beside rung five write two.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.320
At six the counts are two and two, giving four.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:12.400
At seven they give six.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:15.540
At eight, six plus four gives ten.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.410
The zero stays in the log, holding the place of

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:19.380
the broken rung.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:22.080
A brass bell hangs on rung five.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.610
This time the beetle must land on that rung and ring the

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:26.900
bell on its way to eight.

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.160
Which of the ten routes count now?

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.050
Imagine cutting a successful route into two pieces

00:01:32.050 --> 00:01:32.800
at the bell.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.130
Two routes arrive at the bell.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.690
From five to eight the remaining climb is three rungs.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.300
The jumps can be three singles, a single followed by a

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.610
double, or a double followed by a single.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.210
Each beginning accepts all three endings.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.490
Two groups of three give six bell routes.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.240
Why did we add at a landing and multiply at the bell?

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.500
Different final jumps put the routes in separate piles.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:04.050
At a compulsory stop, each beginning joins to

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:05.260
each possible ending.

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.520
Keep a zero for a forbidden landing.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:11.070
If a rule restricts the next jump, record how the route

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.020
arrived as well.
